{:edb/db-before-t 2286 :edb/db-after-t 2287 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535599 50 #inst "2026-10-09T03:20:42.198Z" 13194139535599 true] [17592186198007 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/assets/eec7d2b67f0b76bb8ec6874cf63f8cb09005f451d684924118983ea1aa39269c.png)\n\nThe $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ on the curve $y=\\ln \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right)$ that is closest to the origin (as shown above) satisfies the equation \n\n$$\n2x + \\bbox[3pt, border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAA^2}}=0.\n$$\n\n \nWhat is the missing expression?" 13194139535599 true] [17592186198007 1100 "The $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ on the curve $y=\\ln \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right)$ that is closest to the origin (as shown above) satisfies the equation\n$2x + \\bbox[3pt, border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAA^2}}=0$.\nWhat is the missing expression?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4218/Source/Images/8d6bc7c46e9b7137f43fa5394d60c422.png)" 13194139535599 false] [17592186198007 1145 "Let's consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(0,0)$ and a general point $P(x, y)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS(x, y) &= (x - 0)^{2} + (y - 0)^{2} \\\\\n&= x^{2} + y^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve\n\n$$\ny=\\ln \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right)\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS &= x^{2} + y^{2} \\\\\n&= x^{2} + (\\ln (\\frac{1}{x}))^{2} \\\\\n&= x^{2} + \\ln^{2} (\\frac{1}{x})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS^{′}(x) = 2x + 2\\ln (\\frac{1}{x}) \\cdot (1)/((\\frac{1}{x})) \\cdot (-\\frac{1}{x^{2}}) &= 0 \\\\\n2x - \\frac{2}{x}\\ln (\\frac{1}{x}) &= 0 \\\\\n2x - \\frac{2}{x}(\\ln 1 - \\ln x) &= 0 \\\\\n2x + 2 ln ⁡ x x &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2\\ln x}{x}\n$$" 13194139535599 false] [17592186198007 1145 "Let's consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(0,0)$ and a general point $P(x, y)\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x,y) & =(x-0)^{2}+(y-0)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\ln \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right).$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{x}))}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{ln}^{2}⁡(\\frac{1}{x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{x})\\cdot \\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{x})}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}) & =0 \\\\ 2x-\\frac{2}{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{x}) & =0 \\\\ 2x-\\frac{2}{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) & =0 \\\\ 2x+\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $\\dfrac{2\\ln x}{x}.$" 13194139535599 true] [17592186198008 1100 "Find the $x$-coordinates of the points on the curve $y=x^2$ that are closest to the point $\\left( 0, \\dfrac{3}{2} \\right).$" 13194139535599 true] [17592186198008 1100 "Find the $x$-coordinates of the points on the curve $y=x^2$ that are closest to the point $\\left(0, \\dfrac{3}{2} \\right)$." 13194139535599 false] [17592186198008 1145 "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between\n\n$$\n\\left(0, \\dfrac{3}{2} \\right)\n$$\n\nand a general point $P(x, y)$:\n\n$$\nS = x^2 + \\left(y- \\dfrac{3}{2} \\right)^2\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=x^2$. Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} S(x) &= x^2 + \\left(y- \\dfrac{3}{2} \\right)^2 \\\\[5pt] &= x^2 + \\left(x^2 - \\dfrac{3}{2} \\right)^2 \\\\[5pt] &= x^2 + x^4 -3x^2 + \\dfrac{9}{4} \\\\[5pt] &= x^4 - 2 x^2 + \\dfrac{9}{4}. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS^{′}(x) = 4x^{3} - 4x &= 0 \\\\\n4x(x^{2} - 1) &= 0 \\\\\nx &= 0, ± 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince there are multiple stationary points, we need to use the candidates test to identify the points of minimum distance. We compute the squared distances for each of the stationary points as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS(0) &= (0)^{4} - 2(0)^{2} + \\frac{9}{4} = \\frac{9}{4} \\\\\nS(-1) &= (-1)^{4} - 2(-1)^{2} + \\frac{9}{4} = \\frac{5}{4} \\\\\nS(1) &= (1)^{4} - 2(1)^{2} + \\frac{9}{4} = \\frac{5}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) is minimized when $x=\\pm 1$." 13194139535599 false] [17592186198008 1145 "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $\\left( 0, \\dfrac{3}{2} \\right)$ and a general point $P(x, y)$:\n\n$$\nS = x^2 + \\left(y- \\dfrac{3}{2} \\right)^2\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=x^2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\nS(x) &=  x^2 + \\left(y- \\dfrac{3}{2} \\right)^2  \\[5pt]\n&=  x^2 + \\left( x^2 - \\dfrac{3}{2} \\right)^2 \\[5pt]\n&=  x^2 +  x^4 -3x^2 + \\dfrac{9}{4}\\[5pt]\n&=  x^4 - 2 x^2 + \\dfrac{9}{4}.\\\n}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=4{x}^{3}-4x & =0 \\\\ 4x({x}^{2}-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince there are multiple stationary points, we need to use the candidates test to identify the points of minimum distance. We compute the squared distances for each of the stationary points as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(0) & =(0)^{4}-2(0)^{2}+\\frac{9}{4}=\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ S(-1) & =(-1)^{4}-2(-1)^{2}+\\frac{9}{4}=\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ S(1) & =(1)^{4}-2(1)^{2}+\\frac{9}{4}=\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) is minimized when $x=\\pm 1.$" 13194139535599 true] [17592186198011 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/assets/44ef8fc83ea0a70dcc16769ee6965727ec2c9584e97cc417e34184d633bbf876.png)\n\n  \n\nWhat is the least distance between the curve $y=\\sqrt{2x^2+3}+1$ and the point $(3,1)?$" 13194139535599 true] [17592186198011 1100 "What is the least distance between the curve $y=\\sqrt{2x^2+3}+1$ and the point $(3,1)$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4218/Source/Images/f39063edc015aeb9229c52d9e7752375.png)" 13194139535599 false] [17592186198011 1145 "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(3,1)$ and a general point $P(x, y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS &= (x - 3)^{2} + (y - 1)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{2x^2+3}+1\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS &= (x - 3)^{2} + (y - 1)^{2} \\\\\n&= (x - 3)^{2} + (\\sqrt{2x^{2} + 3} + 1 - 1)^{2} \\\\\n&= (x - 3)^{2} + (\\sqrt{2x^{2} + 3})^{2} \\\\\n&= x^{2} - 6x + 9 + 2x^{2} + 3 \\\\\n&= 3x^{2} - 6x + 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS^{′}(x) = 6x - 6 &= 0 \\\\\n6x &= 6 \\\\\nx &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that\n\n$$\nS''(1) =6 > 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x = 1$ is a minimum of $S$.\n\nSince $S$ gives the squared distance between the two points, the actual distance $d$ between the two points when $x=1$ is given by\n\n$$\nd = \\sqrt{S(1)}\n$$\n\nComputing this quantity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd &= \\sqrt{S(1)} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{3(1)^{2} - 6(1) + 12} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{9} \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535599 false] [17592186198011 1145 "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(3,1)$ and a general point $P(x, y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =(x-3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\sqrt{2x^2+3}+1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =(x-3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-3)^{2}+(\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+3}+1-1)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-3)^{2}+(\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+3})^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-6x+9+2{x}^{2}+3 \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}-6x+12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=6x-6 & =0 \\\\ 6x & =6 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that $S''(1) =6 > 0.$ Therefore, $x = 1$ is a minimum of $S.$\n\nSince $S$ gives the squared distance between the two points, the actual distance $d$ between the two points when $x=1$ is given by\n\n$$\nd = \\sqrt{S(1)}.\n$$\n\nComputing this quantity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{S(1)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3(1)^{2}-6(1)+12} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535599 true] [17592186198173 1020 #edb/ref 17592186356891 13194139535599 true] [17592186198173 1020 #edb/ref 17592186356898 13194139535599 true] [17592186198173 1020 #edb/ref 17592186356952 13194139535599 true] [17592186198173 1020 #edb/ref 17592186356959 13194139535599 true] [17592186198173 1020 #edb/ref 17592186356966 13194139535599 true] [17592186198174 1020 #edb/ref 17592186356905 13194139535599 true] [17592186198174 1020 #edb/ref 17592186356912 13194139535599 true] [17592186198174 1020 #edb/ref 17592186356919 13194139535599 true] [17592186198174 1020 #edb/ref 17592186356936 13194139535599 true] [17592186198174 1020 #edb/ref 17592186356939 13194139535599 true] [17592186198175 1020 #edb/ref 17592186356926 13194139535599 true] [17592186198175 1020 #edb/ref 17592186356933 13194139535599 true] [17592186198175 1020 #edb/ref 17592186356942 13194139535599 true] [17592186198175 1020 #edb/ref 17592186356945 13194139535599 true] [17592186198175 1020 #edb/ref 17592186356949 13194139535599 true] [17592186356891 1017 #edb/ref 17592186356892 13194139535599 true] [17592186356891 1089 "q-118387" 13194139535599 true] [175921