762-b57a-ba562a9cb695" 13194139535599 true] [17592186356926 1145 "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(5,2)$ and a general point $P(x,y)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =(x-5)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\sqrt{2x}+2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =(x-5)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-5)^{2}+(\\sqrt{2x}+2-2)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-5)^{2}+(\\sqrt{2x})^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-10x+25+2x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-8x+25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x-8 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =8 \\\\ x & =\\frac{8}{2} \\\\ x & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(x)=2>0.$ Therefore, $x=4$ is a minimum of $S.$\n\nSince $S$ gives the squared distance between the two points, the actual distance $d$ between the two points when $x=4$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{S(4)}.\n$$\n\nComputing this quantity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{S(4)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}-8(4)+25} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16-32+25} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535599 true] [17592186356927 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356928 13194139535599 true] [17592186356927 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356929 13194139535599 true] [17592186356927 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356930 13194139535599 true] [17592186356927 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356931 13194139535599 true] [17592186356927 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356932 13194139535599 true] [17592186356927 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535599 true] [17592186356927 1041 "selection" 13194139535599 true] [17592186356927 1104 #edb/ref 17592186356931 13194139535599 true] [17592186356927 1110 #uuid "3d925153-91ae-5078-8c6e-4a25bdd237a9" 13194139535599 true] [17592186356928 1034 "2\\sqrt{5}" 13194139535599 true] [17592186356928 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535599 true] [17592186356928 1111 #uuid "b2e48e1c-028c-5f28-98d4-67f9c2a68c03" 13194139535599 