that ${S}^{″}(x)=2>0.$ Therefore, $x=2$ is a minimum of $S.$\n\nSince $S$ gives the squared distance between the two points, the actual distance $d$ between the two points when $x=2$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{S\\,(2)}.\n$$\n\nComputing this quantity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{S\\,(2)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(2)}^{2}-4\\,(2)+15} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4-8+15} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535599 true] [17592186356934 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356935 13194139535599 true] [17592186356934 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535599 true] [17592186356934 1041 "field-1" 13194139535599 true] [17592186356934 1104 #edb/ref 17592186356935 13194139535599 true] [17592186356934 1110 #uuid "ffbbc6de-b10b-5f30-b24e-2c9a843b9a06" 13194139535599 true] [17592186356935 1034 "\\sqrt{11}" 13194139535599 true] [17592186356935 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535599 true] [17592186356935 1111 #uuid "8f68f8b4-85ed-5c0a-af89-c579d97962e4" 13194139535599 true] [17592186356936 1017 #edb/ref 17592186356937 13194139535599 true] [17592186356936 1089 "q-344820" 13194139535599 true] [17592186356936 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535599 true] [17592186356936 1100 "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point on the curve $y=\\sqrt{4x+5}+2$ that is closest to the point $(3,2).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535599 true] [17592186356936 1113 #uuid "70f7604d-a827-588c-b0d6-d2226b6a5f91" 13194139535599 true] [17592186356936 1145 "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(3,2)$ and a general point $P(x,y)$:\n\n$$\nS=(x-3)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\sqrt{4x+5}+2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & =(x-3)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-3)^{2}+(\\sqrt{4x+5}+2-2)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-3)^{2}+4x+5 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-6x+9+4x+5 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-2x+14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x-2 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =2 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(x)=2>0.$ Therefore, $x=1$ is a minimum of $S.$\n\nSo, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) is minimized when $x=1.$" 13194139535599 true] [17592186356937 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356938 13194139535599 true] [17592186356937 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535599 true] [17592186356937 1041 "field-1" 13194139535599 true] [17592186356937 1104 #edb/ref 17592186356938 13194139535599 true] [17592186356937 1110 #uuid "daa8ae59-e10e-5893-a65d-296ad4f59f95" 13194139535599 true] [17592186356938 1034 "1" 13194139535599 true] [17592186356938 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535599 true] [17592186356938 1111 #uuid "2a0dcabf-f85d-507e-ab17-0841cc595107" 13194139535599 true] [17592186356939 1017 #edb/ref 17592186356940 13194139535599 true] [17592186356939 1089 "q-344831" 13194139535599 true] [17592186356939 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535599 true] [17592186356939 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/assets/be3108802cde61c1ffcc5fdf603e97255b4082541f20f628aedd1a01127c05b7.png)\n\nFind the $y$-coordinate of the point on the curve $y=-{x}^{2}+6x$ that is closest to the point $(3,0).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535599 true] [17592186356939 1113 #uuid "0d139748-7409-5b25-b840-b55a718e3b8a" 13194139535599 true] [17592186356939 1145 "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(3,0)$ and a general point $P(x,y)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =(x-3)^{2}+(y-0)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-6x+9+{y}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-6x+{y}^{2}+9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=-{x}^{2}+6x.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(y) & =-y+{y}^{2}+9 \\\\  & ={y}^{2}-y+9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(y)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(y)=2y-1 & =0 \\\\ 2y & =1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(y)=2>0.$ Therefore, $y=\\frac{1}{2}$ is a minimum of $S.$\n\nSo, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) is minimized when $y=\\frac{1}{2}.$" 13194139535599 true] [17592186356940 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356941 13194139535599 true] [17592186356940 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535599 true] [17592186356940 1041 "field-1" 13194139535599 true] [17592186356940 1104 #edb/ref 17592186356941 13194139535599 true] [17592186356940 1110 #uuid "ee543076-7ce5-536d-9e9f-e6e9c432629e" 13194139535599 true] [17592186356941 1034 "\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139535599 true] [17592186356941 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535599 true] [17592186356941 1111 #uuid "38a99c1b-04cd-5a81-84a1-f33e3af185c9" 13194139535599 true] [17592186356942 1017 #edb/ref 17592186356943 13194139535599 true] [17592186356942 1089 "q-344839" 13194139535599 true] [17592186356942 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535599 true] [17592186356942 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/assets/4108c44d3fac12cc38c0aab73ed1600f034ad88c1c50be91ea7bb29145ce4dd7.png)\n\nFind the least distance $d$ between the curve $y=\\sqrt{|x-1|}+3$ and the point $(3,3).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$d=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535599 true] [17592186356942 1113 #uuid "9a185d3d-1d4f-52d3-ba5d-86f674f6bdc0" 13194139535599 true] [17592186356942 1145 "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(3,3)$ and a general point $P(x,y)$:\n\n$$\nS=(x-3)^{2}+(y-3)^{2}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\sqrt{|x-1|}+3.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & =(x-3)^{2}+(y-3)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-3)^{2}+(\\sqrt{|x-1|}+3-3)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-3)^{2}+(\\sqrt{|x-1|})^{2} \\\\  & =(x-3)^{2}+|x-1|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x\\ge 1,$ we have $|x-1|=x-1,$ so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & =(x-3)^{2}+(x-1) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-6x+9+x-1 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-5x+8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x-5 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =5 \\\\ x & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(x)=2>0,$ so $x=\\frac{5}{2}$ gives a minimum of $S.$\n\nSince $S$ gives the squared distance between the two points, the actual distance $d$ between the two points when $x=\\frac{5}{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{S\\,(\\frac{5}{2})}.\n$$\n\nComputing this quantity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{S\\,(\\frac{5}{2})} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}-5(\\frac{5}{2})+8} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535599 true] [17592186356943 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356944 13194139535599 true] [17592186356943 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535599 true] [17592186356943 1041 "field-1" 13194139535599 true] [17592186356943 1104 #edb/ref 17592186356944 13194139535599 true] [17592186356943 1110 #uuid "a28dda0a-005b-59bd-a8d5-bb62b3e91c61" 13194139535599 true] [17592186356944 1034 "\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{2}" 13194139535599 true] [17592186356944 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535599 true] [17592186356944 1111 #uuid "7f6c161a-b194-5006-a476-6011e426dc77" 13194139535599 true] [17592186356945 1017 #edb/ref 17592186356946 13194139535599 true] [17592186356945 1089 "q-344845" 13194139535599 true] [17592186356945 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535599 true] [17592186356945 1100 "Find the least distance between the curve $y=\\sqrt{2x+2}+1$ and the point $P(3,1).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$d=$ \n{{field-1}}" 13194139535599 true] [17592186356945 1113 #uuid "7c3a7702-c807-5572-8902-3d9c8c59af55" 13194139535599 true] [17592186356945 1145 "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(3,1)$ and a general point $P(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =(x-3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\sqrt{2x+2}+1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =(x-3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-3)^{2}+{(\\sqrt{2x+2}+1-1)}^{2} \\\\  & =(x-3)^{2}+(\\sqrt{2x+2})^{2} \\\\  & =(x-3)^{2}+2x+2 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-6x+9+2x+2 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-4x+11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x-4 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =4 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(x)=2>0.$ Therefore, $x=2$ is a minimum of $S.$\n\nSince $S$ gives the squared distance between the two points, the actual distance $d$ between the two points when $x=2$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{S\\,(2)}.\n$$\n\nComputing this quantity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{S\\,(2)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2)^{2}-4(2)+11} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4-8+11} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535599 true] [17592186356946 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356947 13194139535599 true] [17592186356946 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356948 13194139535599 true] [17592186356946 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535599 true] [17592186356946 1041 "field-1" 13194139535599 true] [17592186356946 1104 #edb/ref 17592186356947 13194139535599 true] [17592186356946 1110 #uuid "573eb4f6-5e0c-53c7-b5ee-da0ba26bb767" 13194139535599 true] [17592186356947 1034 "\\sqrt{7}" 13194139535599 true] [17592186356947 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535599 true] [17592186356947 1111 #uuid "e3d7f20f-a379-5e90-8bb6-8ed8178233e1" 13194139535599 true] [17592186356948 1034 "7" 13194139535599 true] [17592186356948 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535599 true] [17592186356948 1111 #uuid "3711f8a8-b24b-5809-9b9d-552f164a5544" 13194139535599 true] [17592186356949 1017 #edb/ref 17592186356950 13194139535599 true] [17592186356949 1089 "q-344846" 13194139535599 true] [17592186356949 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535599 true] [17592186356949 1100 "Find the least distance between the curve $y=\\sqrt{(x-1)^{2}+4}+2$ and the point $P(1,2).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$d=$ \n{{field-1}}" 13194139535599 true] [17592186356949 1113 #uuid "117a1e44-2d63-563a-83d8-0eda966066d2" 13194139535599 true] [17592186356949 1145 "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(1,2)$ and a general point $P(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =(x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\sqrt{(x-1)^{2}+4}+2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =(x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-1)^{2}+{(\\sqrt{(x-1)^{2}+4}+2-2)}^{2} \\\\  & =(x-1)^{2}+(\\sqrt{(x-1)^{2}+4})^{2} \\\\  & =(x-1)^{2}+(x-1)^{2}+4 \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}-4x+6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=4x-4 & =0 \\\\ 4x & =4 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(x)=4>0.$ Therefore, $x=1$ is a minimum of $S.$\n\nSince $S$ gives the squared distance between the two points, the actual distance $d$ between the two points when $x=1$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{S\\,(1)}.\n$$\n\nComputing this quantity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{S\\,(1)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2(1)^{2}-4(1)+6} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2-4+6} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535599 true] [17592186356950 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356951 13194139535599 true] [17592186356950 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535599 true] [17592186356950 1041 "field-1" 13194139535599 true] [17592186356950 1104 #edb/ref 17592186356951 13194139535599 true] [17592186356950 1110 #uuid "fdb10735-f330-546c-8f21-b9c5ed2e23e0" 13194139535599 true] [17592186356951 1034 "2" 13194139535599 true] [17592186356951 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535599 true] [17592186356951 1111 #uuid "c1174c62-c830-5bd5-9187-2dd933ea8280" 13194139535599 true] [17592186356952 1017 #edb/ref 17592186356953 13194139535599 true] [17592186356952 1089 "q-49049" 13194139535599 true] [17592